do $do$
declare p jsonb := $pl$[{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-106-v2","t":"Elements of Life","s":"A nursery grows duckweed, a small aquatic plant, in nutrient solutions. Duckweed uses nucleic acids for genetic information and phospholipids for membranes. One group initially receives too little nitrogen and phosphorus, while all other required elements are sufficient. A first fertilizer treatment eliminates the nitrogen shortage but supplies no phosphorus. A second treatment then restores phosphorus to a sufficient level. Assuming these shortages are the only limits on production and the plants have no other phosphorus source, how will the second treatment affect nucleic acid and phospholipid production?","a":"D","c":[["A","Only phospholipid production will recover because nucleic acid production already recovered when nitrogen became sufficient.",false,"Nitrogen-only nucleic acid trap: This treats nitrogen as the only missing element needed for nucleic acids. Their phosphorus requirement kept production limited after the first treatment, so the second treatment supports their recovery too. Fix: Check both nitrogen and phosphorus availability before predicting nucleic acid recovery."],["B","Only nucleic acid production will recover because phospholipid production was already supported by sufficient carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen.",false,"All-lipids-are-alike trap: This incorrectly applies the elemental composition of fats to phospholipids. Phospholipids also contain phosphorus, so their production remained limited until the second treatment. Fix: Identify the lipid type and check whether it contains phosphorus."],["C","Neither production rate will change because nitrogen from the first treatment already replaced phosphorus in both materials.",false,"Element-substitution trap: This assumes one abundant element can replace a different required element. Nitrogen cannot substitute for phosphorus in nucleic acids or phospholipids, so the first treatment left both phosphorus-limited. Fix: Match each shortage to the specific element supplied by the treatment."],["D","Production of both materials can return to normal because the second treatment supplies an element required by both.",true,"Both nucleic acids and phospholipids contain phosphorus, so both remain limited after the nitrogen treatment. Supplying phosphorus removes the remaining elemental shortage, allowing normal production of both materials."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-106-v3","t":"Elements of Life","s":"An algae-production facility changes the nutrient mixture supplied to its cultures. The original mixture provided adequate amounts of every required element; the replacement supplies twice as much nitrogen but too little phosphorus, with all other required elements still adequate. The algae make nucleic acids for genetic information and phospholipids for membranes. After stored phosphorus is exhausted, and with no other phosphorus source, how should production of these materials compare with production before the change?","a":"A","c":[["A","Production of both materials decreases because the phosphorus shortage restricts construction of both nucleic acids and phospholipids.",true,"Phosphorus is a required building material for both nucleic acids and phospholipids. Although nitrogen remains abundant, the reduced phosphorus supply prevents the algae from maintaining their previous production rates for either material."],["B","Nucleic acid production stays at its previous rate because nitrogen is sufficient, while phospholipid production decreases because phosphorus is scarce.",false,"Nitrogen-only nucleic acid trap: This treats sufficient nitrogen as sufficient material for making nucleic acids. Their production also requires phosphorus, which is now limiting. Fix: Check for both nitrogen and phosphorus before predicting unchanged nucleic acid production."],["C","Phospholipid production stays at its previous rate because carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen are sufficient, while nucleic acid production decreases.",false,"All-lipids-are-alike trap: This overlooks the phosphorus requirement of phospholipids by treating all lipids as containing only carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen. Those abundant elements cannot supply the missing phosphorus. Fix: Identify the lipid subtype and check its required elements."],["D","Production of both materials stays at its previous rate because the extra nitrogen substitutes for the missing phosphorus.",false,"Element-substitution trap: This assumes an excess of one element can compensate for a shortage of another. Nitrogen cannot replace the phosphorus required in nucleic acids or phospholipids. Fix: Match each shortage to its required element rather than adding nutrient supplies together."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-107","t":"Introduction to Macromolecules","s":"A cell breaks a long polymer into shorter chains by cleaving covalent bonds between its subunits. Which statement explains the chemical reaction responsible for this change?","a":"B","c":[["A","Hydrolysis releases water as covalent bonds between subunits break.",false,"Water-direction reversal: this identifies bond cleavage but reverses water’s role. Hydrolysis consumes water rather than producing it. Fix: Place water on the reactant side when modeling hydrolysis."],["B","Hydrolysis consumes water to break covalent bonds between subunits.",true,"Breaking the polymer into shorter chains requires bond cleavage. Hydrolysis uses water in this process, adding H to one newly separated end and OH to the other."],["C","Hydrolysis consumes water to form covalent bonds between subunits.",false,"Bond-change reversal: this identifies water use but confuses bond cleavage with bond formation. Forming bonds between subunits would build a larger molecule, not break the polymer into shorter chains. Fix: Check whether the reaction joins subunits or separates them before describing the bonds."],["D","Dehydration synthesis releases water as covalent bonds form between subunits.",false,"Synthesis–breakdown confusion: this accurately describes dehydration synthesis, but that process builds polymers. The cell is breaking an existing polymer apart, so bond formation cannot explain the change. Fix: Identify whether the molecule is being built or broken before naming the reaction."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-107-v1","t":"Introduction to Macromolecules","s":"A laboratory sample contains a polymer made of 20 subunits. After a reaction, all 20 subunits are present as separate monomers. Which statement explains the chemical process that separated them?","a":"A","c":[["A","Hydrolysis uses water to cleave the covalent bonds linking the monomers.",true,"Producing separate monomers from a polymer requires breaking the bonds that connect its subunits. Hydrolysis supplies H and OH from water to the separated ends as each bond is cleaved."],["B","Hydrolysis produces water when it cleaves the covalent bonds linking the monomers.",false,"Water-direction reversal: this recognizes bond breaking but incorrectly treats water as a product. Hydrolysis requires water as a reactant. Fix: Mark whether water enters or leaves the reaction before describing its role."],["C","Hydrolysis uses water to create covalent bonds linking the monomers.",false,"Bond-change reversal: this recognizes water consumption but substitutes bond formation for bond cleavage. Creating links between monomers would join them rather than separate them. Fix: Compare the starting and ending molecules to determine whether bonds form or break."],["D","Dehydration synthesis produces water when it creates covalent bonds linking the monomers.",false,"Synthesis–breakdown confusion: this describes a joining reaction, not the separation observed in the sample. Dehydration synthesis connects subunits into larger molecules. Fix: Classify the observed change as assembly or disassembly before choosing the reaction name."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-107-v2","t":"Introduction to Macromolecules","s":"A beetle digests a food polymer, producing smaller fragments by separating linked subunits. Which statement explains the chemical reaction that produces these fragments?","a":"C","c":[["A","Hydrolysis produces water while cleaving covalent bonds linking the subunits.",false,"Water-direction reversal: the bond change is identified correctly, but water is incorrectly treated as a product. Hydrolysis requires water as a reactant. Fix: Write water before the reaction arrow when representing hydrolysis."],["B","Hydrolysis uses water to create covalent bonds linking the subunits.",false,"Bond-change reversal: water use is identified correctly, but bond formation cannot account for separating linked subunits. Joining subunits builds a larger molecule. Fix: Compare the starting molecule and products to determine whether bonds form or break."],["C","Hydrolysis uses water to cleave covalent bonds linking the subunits.",true,"Producing smaller fragments requires breaking bonds within the food polymer. Hydrolysis accomplishes this by using water, with H and OH added to the separated ends."],["D","Dehydration synthesis produces water while creating covalent bonds linking the subunits.",false,"Synthesis–breakdown confusion: this describes a reaction that joins subunits rather than separating them. Dehydration synthesis therefore cannot explain the beetle’s production of smaller fragments. Fix: Classify the change as assembly or disassembly before choosing the reaction name."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-107-v3","t":"Introduction to Macromolecules","s":"As a bean seed begins growing, it converts a stored polymer into separate monomers. Which statement explains the reaction that separates the subunits?","a":"B","c":[["A","Hydrolysis produces water when the covalent links connecting the monomers are cleaved.",false,"Water-direction reversal: the bond change is identified correctly, but water is used rather than released during hydrolysis. Fix: Mark water as an input when representing a hydrolysis reaction."],["B","Hydrolysis uses water to cleave the covalent links connecting the monomers.",true,"Producing separate monomers from a polymer requires breaking the bonds that connect its subunits. During hydrolysis, water supplies H and OH, which attach to the ends exposed by bond cleavage."],["C","Hydrolysis uses water to create covalent links connecting the monomers.",false,"Bond-change reversal: water use is identified correctly, but creating links would join subunits rather than separate them. Fix: Compare the starting and ending molecules to determine whether bonds form or break."],["D","Dehydration synthesis produces water when covalent links connecting the monomers are created.",false,"Synthesis–breakdown confusion: this describes polymer building, whereas the seed is separating a polymer into monomers. Dehydration synthesis joins subunits instead of separating them. Fix: Classify the change as building or breakdown before choosing the reaction name."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-108","t":"Introduction to Macromolecules","s":"A researcher tracks polymers and free monomers in a reaction mixture. Each polymer contains six monomers joined by covalent bonds.\n\nTime (minutes) | Number of polymers | Number of free monomers\n0 | 10 | 0\n5 | 5 | 30\n10 | 0 | 60\n\nWhich statement best describes the trend in the data and the reaction responsible?","a":"D","c":[["A","Polymers decrease as free monomers increase; dehydration synthesis releases water to break covalent bonds.",false,"Reaction-name reversal: this identifies breakdown but assigns it to dehydration synthesis. Dehydration synthesis forms covalent bonds between monomers rather than breaking those bonds. Fix: Determine whether bonds are forming or breaking before naming the reaction."],["B","Polymers decrease as free monomers increase; hydrolysis releases water when it breaks covalent bonds.",false,"Water-direction reversal: this correctly identifies hydrolysis but treats water as a product. Hydrolysis consumes water during bond cleavage. Fix: Place water on the reactant side when describing hydrolysis."],["C","Polymers increase as free monomers decrease; dehydration synthesis releases water to form covalent bonds.",false,"Trend reversal: this describes polymer formation, but the table shows polymers decreasing from 10 to 0 and free monomers increasing from 0 to 60. Fix: Compare each column’s final value with its initial value before identifying the reaction."],["D","Polymers decrease as free monomers increase; hydrolysis consumes water to break covalent bonds.",true,"The decrease in polymers and increase in free monomers indicate polymer breakdown. Hydrolysis breaks the bonds between monomers by adding water, supplying H to one resulting end and OH to the other."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-108-v1","t":"Introduction to Macromolecules","s":"A classroom computer simulation represents a cell's supply of free monomers and polymers. Every polymer consists of four monomers connected by covalent bonds.\n\nSimulation step | Free monomers | Polymers\n0 | 48 | 0\n1 | 24 | 6\n2 | 0 | 12\n\nWhich statement accurately connects the changes shown in the table to the reaction occurring?","a":"A","c":[["A","Free monomers decrease while polymers increase; dehydration synthesis forms covalent bonds and releases water.",true,"Free monomers fall from 48 to 0 as polymers rise from 0 to 12, indicating that monomers are being joined into polymers. Dehydration synthesis joins monomers by forming covalent bonds while releasing water."],["B","Free monomers decrease while polymers increase; hydrolysis forms covalent bonds and consumes water.",false,"Reaction-name reversal: this recognizes polymer formation but assigns it to hydrolysis. Hydrolysis uses water to cleave bonds, rather than joining monomers into polymers. Fix: Identify bond formation or cleavage from the data, then match it to the reaction name."],["C","Free monomers decrease while polymers increase; dehydration synthesis forms covalent bonds and consumes water.",false,"Water-direction reversal: this identifies the bond-forming reaction but incorrectly makes water a reactant. Dehydration synthesis releases water as monomers join. Fix: Put water on the product side when representing dehydration synthesis."],["D","Free monomers increase while polymers decrease; hydrolysis breaks covalent bonds and consumes water.",false,"Trend reversal: this describes breakdown, although free monomers decline from 48 to 0 and polymers increase from 0 to 12. The observed changes indicate polymer formation, not hydrolysis. Fix: Mark each column as increasing or decreasing using its first and last values."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-108-v2","t":"Introduction to Macromolecules","s":"A biodegradable coating contains biological polymer chains, each made of eight monomers connected by covalent bonds. During processing, a fixed sample of the coating gives these measurements:\n\nProcessing time (minutes) | Intact polymer chains | Unattached monomers\n0 | 12 | 0\n4 | 9 | 24\n8 | 3 | 72\n\nWhich description connects the measured changes with the chemical reaction taking place?","a":"D","c":[["A","Intact chains become less numerous while unattached monomers accumulate; dehydration synthesis releases water to cleave covalent bonds.",false,"Reaction-name reversal: the observed breakdown is assigned to a reaction that builds polymers. Dehydration synthesis joins monomers through covalent bond formation, so it cannot explain this breakdown. Fix: Identify bond formation or cleavage before choosing the reaction name."],["B","Intact chains become less numerous while unattached monomers accumulate; hydrolysis produces water as it cleaves covalent bonds.",false,"Water-direction reversal: hydrolysis is identified appropriately, but water is incorrectly described as an output. Water is used in hydrolysis, supplying H and OH to the products of bond cleavage. Fix: Mark water as an input when writing a hydrolysis reaction."],["C","Intact chains become more numerous while unattached monomers disappear; dehydration synthesis releases water as it forms covalent bonds.",false,"Trend reversal: the proposed changes run opposite to the measurements. The sample loses nine intact chains and gains 72 unattached monomers rather than showing polymer assembly. Fix: Calculate the change from first to last measurement in each column."],["D","Intact chains become less numerous while unattached monomers accumulate; hydrolysis uses water to cleave covalent bonds.",true,"Chain counts fall from 12 to 3 while unattached monomer counts rise from 0 to 72, indicating polymer breakdown. Hydrolysis accomplishes this by using water to split covalent bonds between monomers."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-108-v3","t":"Introduction to Macromolecules","s":"During growth, a bacterial culture contains free monomers and polymer chains, each consisting of ten monomers connected by covalent bonds. The following measurements come from equal-sized samples of the culture.\n\nTime (minutes) | Free monomers (millions) | Polymer chains (millions)\n0 | 120 | 0\n3 | 80 | 4\n6 | 40 | 8\n\nWhich description correctly links the observed changes to the chemical reaction involved?","a":"A","c":[["A","Free monomers decline while polymer chains become more numerous; dehydration synthesis forms covalent bonds and releases water.",true,"Free monomers fall from 120 million to 40 million while polymer chains rise from zero to 8 million, indicating polymer formation. Dehydration synthesis joins monomers by forming covalent bonds, with water released during each joining reaction."],["B","Free monomers decline while polymer chains become more numerous; hydrolysis forms covalent bonds and consumes water.",false,"Reaction-name reversal: the trend indicates assembly, but hydrolysis is assigned the bond-building role. Hydrolysis uses water to cleave bonds rather than join monomers. Fix: Identify whether monomers are joining or separating, then select the matching reaction name."],["C","Free monomers decline while polymer chains become more numerous; dehydration synthesis forms covalent bonds and consumes water.",false,"Water-direction reversal: polymer formation and the reaction name are identified appropriately, but water is treated as an input. Dehydration synthesis produces water as monomers join. Fix: Put water on the product side when representing dehydration synthesis."],["D","Free monomers become more numerous while polymer chains decline; hydrolysis breaks covalent bonds and consumes water.",false,"Trend reversal: the chemistry describes breakdown, but the measured changes show assembly. The monomer count decreases by 80 million, while the chain count increases by 8 million. Fix: Mark each column’s change as an increase or decrease before interpreting the reaction."]]}]$pl$::jsonb; r jsonb; c jsonb; v_ci uuid; v_civ uuid; n int;
begin
  if jsonb_array_length(p) <> 10 then raise exception 'chunk size'; end if;
  if exists (select 1 from app.content_items ci where ci.exam_pack_version_id='2d88ba5e-a6a3-43b8-bfae-9e5505a178a7' and ci.content_key in (select x->>'k' from jsonb_array_elements(p) x))
    then raise exception 'chunk already loaded'; end if;
  for r in select * from jsonb_array_elements(p) loop
    insert into app.content_items (exam_pack_version_id, content_key, item_type, title, status)
    values ('2d88ba5e-a6a3-43b8-bfae-9e5505a178a7', r->>'k', 'mcq', r->>'t', 'draft') returning id into v_ci;
    insert into app.content_item_versions (content_item_id, version_num, stem, stimulus, content_hash, status, review_status, canonical_answer_1)
    values (v_ci, 1, r->>'s', null, md5(r->>'k'), 'draft', 'tutor_review_pending', r->>'a') returning id into v_civ;
    for c in select * from jsonb_array_elements(r->'c') loop
      insert into app.mcq_choices (content_item_version_id, choice_key, choice_text, is_correct, rationale)
      values (v_civ, c->>0, c->>1, (c->>2)::boolean, c->>3);
    end loop;
  end loop;
  select count(*) into n from app.content_items ci join app.content_item_versions v on v.content_item_id=ci.id join app.mcq_choices m on m.content_item_version_id=v.id
   where ci.exam_pack_version_id='2d88ba5e-a6a3-43b8-bfae-9e5505a178a7' and ci.status='draft' and ci.content_key in (select x->>'k' from jsonb_array_elements(p) x);
  if n <> 40 then raise exception 'chunk 3: expected 40 choice rows, got %', n; end if;
  raise notice 'chunk 3: loaded 10 draft items';
end
$do$;
